\section{Testing and Validation}
The compiler was tested using both valid and invalid input scripts.

\subsection{Valid Input}
Correctly structured scripts produced expected tokens, passed all grammar rules, and generated piano rolls without errors.

\subsection{Invalid Input}
We intentionally introduced errors (e.g., missing \texttt{for}, wrong note like \texttt{H9}) to verify error detection. The compiler returned meaningful exceptions that helped in debugging.

\subsection{Token Verification}
Sample input:
\begin{lstlisting}
play D#4 for 1.5 at 6.0
\end{lstlisting}

Generated tokens:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \texttt{KEYWORD(play)}
    \item \texttt{NOTE(D\#4)}
    \item \texttt{KEYWORD(for)}
    \item \texttt{NUMBER(1.5)}
    \item \texttt{KEYWORD(at)}
    \item \texttt{NUMBER(6.0)}
\end{itemize}

\section{Discussion}
PianoDSL simplifies how users interact with music and programming. The consistent format promotes predictability, while the graphical output reinforces musical structure. Though basic, the interpreter demonstrates compiler theory applied in a creative and accessible context.

\section{Conclusion}
The project successfully integrates lexical analysis, parsing, and visualization into a unified tool. PianoDSL proves that a pedagogical compiler project can go beyond simple math or shapes and tap into musical creativity.

\section{Folder Structure and Execution}
The project is composed of the following Python modules:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \texttt{lexical.py}: Handles token recognition.
    \item \texttt{sintactic.py}: Validates syntax using grammar rules.
    \item \texttt{semantic.py}: Extracts note data from tokens.
    \item \texttt{compiler.py}: Integrates all components and generates the visualization.
\end{itemize}

To execute the project:
\begin{lstlisting}[language=bash]
python compiler.py
\end{lstlisting}

